## Why an index and not just a folder

A folder answers "what do we have"; a bid needs "**what proves this requirement, and is it still
valid**". Those are different questions, and the second one is where bids fail: a case study that
is one year outside a three-year window, a certificate that lapsed, a named referee who left.
Each row below carries the date and the constraint, so the check is a lookup rather than a memory.

## 1. Index

| ID | Asset | Category | What it proves | Dated | In-window? | Constraints | Used for |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| A-001 | `case_studies/<file>` | methodology / case study / CV / credential / diagram / financial | <the specific claim it can back, not the title> | <YYYY-MM> | <vs this RFP's recency rule> | <client permission? anonymised? NDA?> | R-0xx |

Paths are relative to `01_pursuit/_shared/` — `approaches/` · `case_studies/` · `cvs/` ·
`credentials/` · `diagrams/` · `finance/`. What belongs there versus in `_sources/` is decided
by **where the document came from**, not its subject: see `README.md` beside this file.

**Category** is what the asset *is*; **What it proves** is what it can be cited for. A case study
whose "what it proves" restates its title has not been read — write the claim a evaluator would
score, e.g. "end-to-end DWH assessment for a regulated utility, delivered in 12 weeks".

**In-window** is checked against *this* tender's rule, not in the abstract. Restate the rule at
the top of §2 so the column has a meaning.

