## 3. Gaps — what we do NOT hold

The point of an index is that it makes absence visible. List what this bid needs and we cannot
evidence, so it becomes a research task or an upload request rather than a silent hole.

| Need | Requirement | Why nothing here covers it | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| <e.g. utility-sector reference within 3 yrs> | R-0xx | <the nearest asset and why it falls short> | research / request / **cut the claim** |

## Guardrails

- **An asset is not evidence until someone has read it.** "We have a case study on that" is how a
  win-theme gets built on a document that turns out to say something else.
- **Client permission is a real constraint.** A case study naming a client may need their consent
  to cite; an anonymised version may be the only usable form. Record which, per row.
- **Firm assets are ours; sourced material is not.** Anything from `_sources/engagement/` stays
  out of a bid regardless of how useful it looks — see `_sources/README.md`.
- **Update on use, not on schedule.** When a bid finds a gap or discovers an asset is stale, fix
  the row then — an index nobody trusts is worse than no index.
